  diagram:table('The estimate and the reference answer different questions','The reference is available only for offline evaluation, not as a filter input.',['Quantity','Meaning'],[['Predicted mean','What the motion model expected'],['Q','Added variance of motion-model error'],['R','Variance of measurement error'],['Reference trajectory','Independent evaluation of actual simulated position']],'The filter does not get to read the reference position while making its estimate.'),
  parts:[
    p('An uncertainty number is not an observed error','The covariance comes from the estimator’s model and its supplied parameters. Actual error compares an estimate with a reference state. The two can disagree. A filter can return very small covariance while an unmodelled acceleration or sensor bias leaves its mean far away.\n\nIn the simulation we know the reference trajectory. With hardware, obtaining a trustworthy reference and accounting for its own uncertainty is an additional measurement problem.',null,'Do not put reference truth into the inference branch to make the result look better. It belongs in evaluation.'),
    p('Motion uncertainty and measurement uncertainty are different explanations','Q represents the motion-model error added during an interval. R describes error in the observation relative to its measurement model. An unexpected acceleration can violate a constant-velocity assumption; a poorly calibrated detector can shift every reported position. These require investigation of different parts of the system.\n\nIncreasing Q usually gives an observation more influence in our scalar model. Increasing R usually gives it less. Neither operation proves that its new uncertainty is calibrated.',null,'Persistent signed innovations suggest something systematic, but innovations alone do not uniquely identify which model is wrong.'),
    p('Follow the consequence of understating motion error','Our model predicts movement of1m, while the simulated part actually reaches3m. The observation also reports3m. If the prior variance stays very small, the filter resists the useful observation and remains behind it.', 'earlier mean = 0 m; P = 0.1 m²\nexpected displacement = 1 m; Q = 0.01 m²\nmeasurement = 3 m; R = 0.1 m²\n\npredicted mean = 1 m; P⁻ = 0.11 m²\nK = 0.11 / 0.21 ≈ 0.523810\ncorrected mean ≈ 2.047619 m\ncorrected variance ≈ 0.052381 m²', 'The remaining reference error is large relative to the declared spread. Inspect the motion assumption that produced that confidence.'),
    p('Compare squared error with the declared variance','Divide squared reference error by the scalar variance to express error relative to declared uncertainty. We also show whether the reference falls within two declared standard deviations. These are diagnostics, not an automatic accept/reject rule for a robot.\n\nWith a correct scalar Gaussian error model, a two-standard-deviation interval has a known probability interpretation. A handful of hand-picked frames cannot establish that interpretation for a deployed detector and tracker.', 'error = estimate - reference\nnormalised squared error = error² / declared variance\ninside two standard deviations when:\nabs(error) <= 2 × sqrt(declared variance)', 'A very wide interval can cover the reference while providing little useful precision. Assess interval width and error together.'),
    p('Time changes the process model, not only the displayed position','A position-and-velocity model can treat acceleration over an interval as a random constant with known variance. The corresponding process covariance has position/velocity cross terms. This is different from a continuous-time white-acceleration model, which produces other powers and coefficients. State which interpretation is being used.', 'state = [position,velocity]\nF = [[1,dt],[0,1]]\nG = [[dt²/2],[dt]]\nQ = G × acceleration_variance × Gᵀ\n\nP⁻ = F P Fᵀ + Q', 'Acceleration variance here has units m²/s⁴ and refers to a random constant over that interval. It is not a continuous-time spectral density.'),
    p('Correlation and selection can undermine the simple uncertainty story','Two detectors using the same image may share errors. Applying both as independent observations can count information twice. A gate also selects which observations reach the filter, changing the distribution of the retained innovations.\n\nA bimodal location belief, such as uncertainty about which of two aisles contains a robot, is poorly described by a single Gaussian around the midpoint. Nonlinear models and several competing hypotheses may need another approximation or representation.',null,'An extended Kalman filter linearises nonlinear relationships; that approximation is not a guarantee that the full posterior remains Gaussian. Alternative filters also have assumptions and costs.'),
    p('Evaluate an uncertainty repair on a new trajectory','Use held-out motion patterns, gaps, detector failures and identity changes. Compare signed errors and innovation patterns as well as coverage and uncertainty width. If ground truth is available, keep its acquisition independent of the estimator being assessed.\n\nChoose a motion-model change or noise-statistics adjustment for a stated reason, then repeat the evaluation. One covered fresh case is useful feedback, but it does not prove calibration across conditions.',null,'A repair should earn its improvement on trajectories that were not used to choose its parameters.'),
  ],
